ealth programs administration and funding"],"sponsorBioguideId":"S000522","cosponsorCount":109,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/7","industries":[{"industry":"insurance","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly restricts what health insurance plans may cover and how federal subsidies interact with abortion coverage in qualified health plans."},{"industry":"health-providers","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill prohibits federal health care facilities and federal employees from providing abortion services, affecting facilities that currently receive federal funds."},{"industry":"ideological-single-issue","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Organizations focused on reproductive health policy and abortion access or restrictions have a direct stake in the regulatory framework this bill establishes."}],"plainSummary":"This bill would make permanent the restrictions on federal funding for abortion services and health insurance coverage that includes abortion. Currently, these restrictions are renewed each year through appropriations bills; the bill would apply them across all federal programs and funds. It would also prohibit qualified health plans offered through insurance marketplaces from covering abortion. The bill includes exceptions for cases of rape, incest, or when the pregnant person's life is in danger.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"Anyone who buys health insurance. Anyone who pays a medical bill.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"B001260","score":0.061260165720508956},"overlapCount":107},"meaning":{"percentile":20.391723266466595,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[],"unattributedShare":0.9086972455337149}},{"billId":"119-hr-4235","bioguideId":"S001196","cycle":2026,"score":0.003510628265215676,"matches":[{"industry":"legal","donorAmount":18500,"donorShare":0.006195226350380605,"billConfidence":0.85,"contribution":0.003510628265215676}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"llm","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.9086972455337149},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-4235","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"4235","title":"To clarify the Holocaust Expropriated Art Recovery Act of 2016, to appropriately limit the application of defenses based on the passage of time and other non-merits defenses to claims under that Act.","introducedDate":"2025-06-27","latestActionDate":"2025-06-27","latestActionText":"Referred to the House Committee on the Judiciary.","policyArea":"Law","subjects":["Art, artists, authorship","Civil actions and liability","Conflicts and wars","Crime victims","Historical and cultural resources","Museums, exhibitions, cultural centers","War crimes, genocide, crimes against humanity","World history"],"sponsorBioguideId":"L000597","cosponsorCount":29,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/4235","industries":[{"industry":"legal","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"The bill expands litigation procedures, jurisdictional reach, and limits defenses in civil claims, directly affecting law firms handling art recovery and international litigation."},{"industry":"media-entertainment","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Museums and cultural institutions holding artwork are subject to expanded liability and jurisdictional reach under the modified claims procedures."}],"plainSummary":"This bill removes the 2026 deadline for filing lawsuits to recover art and property stolen during the Holocaust, allowing claims to be filed indefinitely as long as they are brought within six years of discovering the item. It makes it easier to sue foreign governments in U.S. courts and to serve legal papers on defendants anywhere in the country. The bill also blocks certain legal defenses, such as arguments that too much time has passed or that U.S. courts should defer to foreign laws, for both current and future cases.","classificationMethod":"llm","plain":{"whoItTouches":"People who have been the victim of a crime.","confidence":"crs-summary"},"topOverlap":{"bioguideId":"N000002","score":0.032950606363763804},"overlapCount":28},"meaning":{"percentile":16.2217659137577,"median":0.010636331736930488,"n":6331,"ordinary":[{"kind":"state-industry","text":"Lawyers & Lobbyists is also the top donor sector for 2 other members from NY. That points to the industry being big in the state, not to anything about this member."}],"unattributedShare":0.9086972455337149}},{"billId":"119-hr-5688","bioguideId":"S001196","cycle":2026,"score":0.0025000307639466935,"matches":[{"industry":"construction","donorAmount":15000,"donorShare":0.005023156500308598,"billConfidence":0.5,"contribution":0.0017321229311408962},{"industry":"transport","donorAmount":3500,"donorShare":0.0011720698500720063,"billConfidence":0.95,"contribution":0.0007679078328057972}],"method":{"formula":"score = Σ_industries ( donorShare(industry) × billWeight(industry) ), where billWeight = classifierConfidence / Σ classifierConfidence. Range 0–1, read as a weighted share of disclosed itemized money.","donorProfileCycle":2026,"billClassificationMethod":"keyword-fallback","unclassifiedDonorShare":0.9086972455337149},"bill":{"id":"119-hr-5688","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"5688","title":"Non-Domiciled CDL Integrity Act","introducedDate":"2025-10-03","latestActionDate":"2026-03-18","latestActionText":"Ordered to be Reported (Amended) by the Yeas and Nays: 35 - 26.","policyArea":"Transportation and Public Works","subjects":["Immigration status and procedures","Licensing and registrations","Motor carriers","State and local government operations"],"sponsorBioguideId":"R000603","cosponsorCount":74,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.c